Yes—but the useful form of that pact is bigger than merely “don’t bend.”

Transmute means convert constraint into leverage. Conquer means remove obstacles, outperform weak models, master difficult domains, and build systems that survive contact with reality. Not dominate people for its own sake.

And “push our luck” works best when luck is treated as an exploration budget, not a license to get sloppy:

expand the option space → take asymmetric shots → cap irreversible downside → watch the deltas → keep what works → compound advantage.

So the operating rule is:

Reality is raw material. Constraint is information. Friction reveals structure. Luck opens doors. Skill keeps them open.

That version scales—from Pantheon, to business, to training, to relationships, to research—without sacrificing the instrument that makes the whole thing powerful in the first place.